  <h1>Kohn–Sham Density Functional Theory</h1>
  <p class="lede">Kohn–Sham theory replaces the interacting ground-state problem by an auxiliary system of noninteracting particles chosen to reproduce the same ground-state density.</p>
  <p>The replacement is exact in principle if the exact exchange–correlation functional is known. In practice, the auxiliary equations become useful only after an approximation for that functional and a numerical self-consistent solution are supplied.</p>

  <h2>The auxiliary system preserves the density, not the interacting wavefunction</h2>
  <p>The Kohn–Sham construction does not claim that real electrons cease to interact. It introduces orbitals whose occupied density equals the density of the interacting ground state:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="density equals sum over states of occupation times orbital magnitude squared">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder>
          <msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\sum_i f_i|\phi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)|^2.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The occupations <math class="math-inline" aria-label="f sub i"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_i</annotation></semantics></math> encode how the auxiliary states contribute to the density. Integer occupations are sufficient for many finite insulating ground states; metals, spin polarization, degeneracy, and finite-temperature formulations require additional care.</p>

  <h2>The universal functional is reorganized</h2>
  <p>Kohn–Sham theory separates the universal functional into a noninteracting kinetic term, a classical Hartree term, and the remaining exchange–correlation contribution:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="F of n equals T s of n plus E H of n plus E x c of n">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[n]=T_s[n]+E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;[n]+E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p><math class="math-inline" aria-label="T s"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_s</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum kinetic energy of noninteracting orbitals yielding the density. The Hartree term is the classical Coulomb self-energy of the density. Everything not captured by those two pieces is assigned to <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E x c"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>

  <h2>The Kohn–Sham equations</h2>
  <p>Stationarity of the energy with respect to orthonormal orbitals gives one-particle equations:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="minus one half laplacian plus effective potential acting on phi i equals epsilon i phi i">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mo>[</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo>
          <msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-\tfrac12\nabla^2+v_s[n](\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)]\phi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\varepsilon_i\phi_i(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The effective potential is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="v s equals v external plus v Hartree plus v exchange correlation">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ext</mi></msub>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_s[n]=v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;ext&#125;&#125;+v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;[n]+v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Because the potential depends on the density reconstructed from the orbitals, this is a nonlinear eigenvalue problem. The equations must therefore be solved self-consistently; the numerical iteration is treated on the <a href={href('/theory/self-consistent-field-methods/')}>Self-Consistent Field Methods</a> page.</p>

  <h2>What the orbitals and eigenvalues do—and do not—mean</h2>
  <p>Kohn–Sham orbitals are auxiliary variables that reproduce the ground-state density and the noninteracting kinetic contribution. They are not, in general, the natural orbitals of the interacting system and should not be interpreted as classical electron trajectories.</p>
  <p>The exact highest occupied eigenvalue has a special relation to the ionization energy under the exact functional and suitable conditions. This does not make the entire Kohn–Sham spectrum a quasiparticle spectrum. Unoccupied eigenvalues, band gaps, and excitation energies generally require additional theory or carefully justified approximations.</p>

  <h2>Total energy is not the sum of occupied eigenvalues</h2>
  <p>The eigenvalue sum contains the effective Hartree and exchange–correlation potentials already sampled by the orbitals. For the ordinary local multiplicative Kohn–Sham form, the electronic total energy can be rearranged as</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Kohn Sham energy equals eigenvalue sum minus Hartree energy plus exchange correlation energy minus integral of exchange correlation potential times density">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>E</mi><mi>KS</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo>
          <mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>r</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;KS&#125;&#125;=\sum_i f_i\varepsilon_i-E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;[n]+E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n]-\int v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;[n](\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,d^3r.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>A nucleus–nucleus term is added separately when the full clamped-nuclei energy is required. The identity above also assumes a local multiplicative XC potential; generalized Kohn–Sham and explicitly nonlocal orbital-dependent operators require their own energy bookkeeping. Comparing raw eigenvalue sums between calculations is therefore not a substitute for comparing consistently evaluated total energies.</p>

  <h2>Spin, occupations, and ensembles extend the basic construction</h2>
  <p>Spin-density functional theory replaces one density by spin-resolved densities or a magnetization field. Fractional occupations may represent ensembles, metallic filling, degeneracy, or finite electronic temperature. These extensions change the variables and variational object and should not be treated as mere numerical conveniences.</p>

  <h2>Where errors enter in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Functional approximation:</strong> the exact exchange–correlation functional is unknown.</li>
    <li><strong>Finite representation:</strong> orbitals and densities are expanded on a basis or grid.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-consistency error:</strong> the nonlinear fixed point is solved only to finite tolerance.</li>
    <li><strong>Sampling and boundary error:</strong> periodic systems require finite cells and Brillouin-zone integration.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These errors are distinct. A tightly converged SCF loop cannot compensate for an unsuitable functional or an incomplete numerical representation.</p>

  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/electronic-structure/kohnsham-auxiliary-system/15B910D74BA0ABE27F512EF4D5E5CF50">Richard M. Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Chapter 7</a> is the main reference for the auxiliary-system construction and its separation from general DFT foundations and later approximations.</p>
  <p><a href="https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRev.140.A1133">Kohn and Sham, “Self-Consistent Equations Including Exchange and Correlation Effects”</a> is the original formulation. It is a historical primary source, not a modern numerical tutorial.</p>
  <p>The second module of École Polytechnique’s <a href="https://www.coursera.org/learn/density-functional-theory"><cite>Density Functional Theory</cite></a> course gives a guided route from a density functional to the auxiliary Kohn–Sham system, the XC term, the Kohn–Sham equations, the effective potential, and the relation to Hartree theory. It is useful for readers who want a lecture sequence rather than the original paper alone. The course is advanced and platform access can require enrolment; neither its auxiliary-system discussion nor a converged SCF cycle licenses an interpretation of every Kohn–Sham eigenvalue as a quasiparticle energy.</p>
  <p><a href="https://lcqs.unistra.fr/teaching/">Laboratoire de Chimie Quantique de Strasbourg / University of Strasbourg, <cite>Kohn–Sham DFT</cite> teaching route</a> is a laboratory-maintained M1 sequence with course notes, three linked video parts, a density-functional-approximations continuation, and a discussion session. It is a directly accessible alternative for readers who want the auxiliary-system derivation and later approximations separated into inspectable teaching units. The route is chemistry-oriented and does not replace periodic-solid discretization, representability analysis, exchange–correlation validation, or material-specific numerical convergence.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page defines the Kohn–Sham auxiliary system and the meaning of its variables. It does not rank exchange–correlation approximations, prescribe mixing parameters, or choose a basis. Those responsibilities belong to the linked XC, SCF, and discretization pages.</p>

